os-dev-report
{
  "issue": 16173,
  "status": "blocked",
  "branch": "claude/issue-16173-shard-timings-refresh",
  "pr": null,
  "premise_still_valid": true,
  "summary": "BLOCKED on the one input the refresh cannot be performed without, and it is a container/egress fact, not a seat or token fact: the eighteen test-core-run-summary-N-of-6 artifacts cannot be downloaded here. GET /repos/objectstack-ai/objectstack/actions/artifacts/{id}/zip answers 302 to productionresultssa1.blob.core.windows.net and the session egress proxy answers 403 to CONNECT; the agent-proxy README classes that as an organization policy denial and says to report it rather than retry or route around it. Confirmed four ways: two distinct artifacts on two distinct runs by curl, the same denial on the pre-signed SAS URL the MCP download_workflow_run_artifact call hands back (so it is the host, not the credential), and the job-logs endpoint, which redirects to the same host. The REST API itself is healthy (repo endpoints 200 throughout), so this is not an access problem that a different token fixes. This is the same blocker already filed as #16222 on 2026-09-06 by the previous devx seat; the dispatch did not clear it and this container has the same policy. ⛔ Nothing was hand-edited, nothing was fabricated from job logs, and no PR was opened, because with the dataset unreachable there is nothing to put in one: rulings 3 and 5 both depend on the regenerated file, and ruling 4 resolves to 'leave it' on its own terms (below). Everything reachable without egress was done and is recorded here so the next actor starts at the download and not before it: the input set is chosen and verified by evidence, the BEFORE bins are measured, the drift-step branch is decided, and one separable defect in the generator itself was found, proven and filed.",
  "inputs_verified_but_unreachable": {
    "rule_applied": "ruling 1, verified rather than assumed: the newest complete push runs on main (workflow 224644133) whose six Test Core (N/6) jobs all concluded success AND whose test-core-run-summary-N-of-6 artifacts are still retained (retention 1 day).",
    "finding": "Five runs pass the literal criterion at 03:1xZ, but two of them are cache replays and would contribute almost nothing: the generator refuses any task whose cache.status is not MISS. Reading each shard's own 'Run this shard's tests' step window (from the jobs API, no egress needed) separates them cleanly. So the brief's three named runs are confirmed as the correct inputs on evidence, not merely re-copied.",
    "chosen": [
      {
        "run": 34070845319,
        "created": "2026-09-07T00:45:59Z",
        "sha": "ac76425f0b",
        "test_step_seconds": "539/380/560/338/1521/1031",
        "all_six": "success",
        "artifacts": "6/6 retained"
      },
      {
        "run": 34069255842,
        "created": "2026-09-07T00:15:55Z",
        "sha": "3e7ef9c238",
        "test_step_seconds": "541/310/504/327/1426/1182",
        "all_six": "success",
        "artifacts": "6/6 retained"
      },
      {
        "run": 34066722126,
        "created": "2026-09-06T23:22:51Z",
        "sha": "0a038cc06d",
        "test_step_seconds": "647/384/600/531/1600/1265",
        "all_six": "success",
        "artifacts": "6/6 retained"
      }
    ],
    "rejected_though_newer": [
      {
        "run": 34077595330,
        "created": "2026-09-07T02:50:05Z",
        "why": "cache replay: shards 2 and 4 ran their test step for 98s and 57s against a 672s prediction (0.15x, 0.08x) — those tasks are cache HITs the generator refuses, so this run measures only part of the workspace."
      },
      {
        "run": 34073696737,
        "created": "2026-09-07T01:38:57Z",
        "why": "cache replay: shard 2 test step 7s, shard 4 60s (0.01x, 0.09x)."
      },
      {
        "run": 34072619046,
        "created": "2026-09-07T01:18:52Z",
        "why": "cache replay: shards 2, 3, 5 and 6 all ~1 minute of job time."
      },
      {
        "run": 34072481948,
        "created": "2026-09-07T01:16:12Z",
        "why": "zero Test Core jobs — the paths filter skipped the leg, so no artifacts exist."
      }
    ],
    "deadline": "artifact retention is 1 day, so the 23:22Z run expires around 2026-09-07T23:22Z and the other two shortly after. After that this exact input set is gone and a new one must be selected by the same rule.",
    "exact_recipe_for_whoever_has_the_channel": "download the six test-core-run-summary-N-of-6 artifacts of each of the three runs above (eighteen files, unzip each to its .turbo/runs/*.json), then run exactly: node scripts/measure-test-shard-timings.mjs PATHS --out scripts/test-shard-timings.json — where PATHS is the eighteen summary paths. ⚠️ Read #16473 first: it changes the ORDER that command should be given its arguments, and what the resulting @objectstack/cli weight actually means."
  },
  "before_measurements_banked": {
    "dataset_on_main": "scripts/test-shard-timings.json measuredAt 2026-08-24, 71 measured packages, secondsPerTestFileFallback 1.522.",
    "predicted_bins_before": "bins 672/672/672/671/672/671s; mean 672s; max/mean 1.00x of the 1.3x bound; floor 404s; 72 shard items from 79 packages (71 measured, 7 estimated from test-file count, @objectstack/dogfood excluded as ci.yml does).",
    "shard_5_before": "@objectstack/cli 1/2, objectql, driver-sqlite-wasm, http-conformance, plugin-reports, service-storage, example-showcase, plugin-pinyin-search, connector-mcp, client-react, observability, trigger-api — 12 items, 672.0s predicted.",
    "shard_6_before": "@objectstack/cli 2/2, plugin-auth, plugin-sharing, driver-mongodb, client, service-messaging, example-embed-objectql, core, connector-openapi, service-job, service-cluster-redis, account, console, docs, example-multi-package, refd-timer-testkit, setup, studio — 18 items, 671.3s predicted.",
    "staleness_quantified": "measured/predicted per shard on the three chosen runs, using each shard's own test-step window (the tighter reading the drift gate uses), predictions 672/672/672/671/672/671s: run 34070845319 → 0.80/0.57/0.83/0.50/2.26/1.54x; run 34069255842 → 0.81/0.46/0.75/0.49/2.12/1.76x; run 34066722126 → 0.96/0.57/0.89/0.79/2.38/1.89x. ⇒ shards 5 and 6, the two carrying the CLI slices, are the only ones above 1.0x and both exceed MAX_MEASURED_OVER_PREDICTED = 1.5 on all three runs. The withheld drift step would red today on exactly those two shards, which is precisely what its own note in ci.yml predicts.",
    "no_2x_disagreement_between_runs": "ruling suggestion checked: across the three runs no shard disagrees with another by more than 2x (the widest is shard 4, 327s vs 531s = 1.62x). Per-package disagreement cannot be checked without the summaries; the cache-replay explanation the brief names is visible at the SHARD level in the two rejected runs above."
  },
  "drift_step_ruling_4": {
    "verdict": "more than a one-line enable ⇒ left to #16465, per ruling 4. ci.yml is NOT touched by this round at all.",
    "evidence": "grep 'check-drift' .github/workflows/ci.yml = 2 hits, both inside the prose note at :696-:720; commented-out step bodies in that block (lines matching a commented '- name:' or 'run:') = 0; Test Core steps whose name matches drift = 0. The note asks for a step to be AUTHORED (argv over .turbo/runs/*.json, a --label, no if:, no continue-on-error, placed above the attestation pair), not for a line to be uncommented.",
    "sibling_branch_state": "recorded even though it is now moot: both flight branches are pushed — claude/issue-16453-merge-group-affected-set at 05cb57b43c and claude/issue-16455-nightly-e2e-live-tiers at d2fab7ab77. No git merge-tree proof was run because this round produces no ci.yml diff to prove anything about."
  },
  "tests": "No code changed, so this is verification of the tree and of the inputs, not of a diff. (1) Worktree ../objectstack-issue-16173-refresh cut from origin/main 5a9138703d (BASE recorded), pnpm install exit 0 in 7.8s, empty branch pushed first as the write-routing probe — push exit 0, so writes route. (2) node scripts/partition-test-shards.mjs --self-test → exit 0, verdict line: 'partition-test-shards: self-test OK (71 measured packages -> 72 shard items, 6 shards, max/mean 1.00x <= 1.3x, floor 404s, bins 672/672/672/671/672/671s)'. (3) node scripts/measure-test-shard-timings.mjs --self-test → exit 0, verdict line: 'measure-test-shard-timings: self-test OK'. Both exits captured before any pipe (redirect first, then $?, then tail). (4) partitioner --shard 5/6 and 6/6 run live against a fresh turbo ls (79 packages, exit 0) for the BEFORE half of ruling 5 — quoted above. (5) The AFTER half, the pin moves, the regenerated dataset and every gate that would grade it are NOT MEASURED, and are reported as NOT MEASURED rather than as passes: they all require the file the download would produce. (6) Egress denial reproduced on artifact 10000503382 (run 34070845319 shard 4) and artifact 10002715965 (run 34077595330 shard 1) — curl (56) CONNECT tunnel failed, response 403, agent-proxy naming productionresultssa1.blob.core.windows.net connect_rejected; and on GET /actions/jobs/101587809709/logs, 302 to the same host. (7) The #16473 defect was proven by driving the exported buildDataset directly with synthetic slice inputs — sliced package recorded 1000s where the median of the three runs fed is 600s, while an unsliced control fed the same three values correctly recorded 300s; the splice case recorded 800s with skippedIncompleteSlices empty. That repro is a scratch file, not committed. No ablation was needed or performed — nothing was mutated on disk.",
  "mcp_calls": "2 — actions_get(download_workflow_run_artifact) to test whether the MCP channel serves artifact bytes (it returns a URL on the denied host, so it does not), and one targeted search_issues for the pre-filing dedup sweep. ⚠️ Channel switch declared: everything else went over repo-scoped REST, which probed 200; the REST search path is NOT available to this session (GET /search/issues → 403 \"sessions are bound to their configured repositories. Use repository-scoped endpoints\"), which is why the dedup sweep used one targeted MCP search rather than a REST list. Its control was live: the query returned 5 open issues including this card, #16222, #16445, #16464 and #16465, so an empty result was not being read as an absence.",
  "open_questions": [
    {
      "question": "How should the eighteen run summaries reach a generator run, given that the artifact host is denied to every agent seat in this container and the local refresh path is out of reach here? This is the only thing standing between the card and its fix, and it is PM/maintainer-side.",
      "options": [
        "A. One-shot manual hand-off: a maintainer (or any seat with browser egress) downloads the six artifacts of run 34070845319 — and ideally also 34069255842 and 34066722126 — and attaches them, or simply runs the one documented command locally and pushes the regenerated file for this seat to finish the pins and the PR against. Unblocks today; expires with the 1-day artifact retention; leaves the loop manual.",
        "B. Land #16464 first (the scheduled refresh workflow, already filed and queued by this dispatching seat) and let the refresh happen IN-RUNNER, where .turbo/runs/ is already on disk and no download exists to be denied. Removes the channel from the loop permanently instead of working around it; costs one card of latency while #16445 keeps the wall at 45.",
        "C. Ask for the egress policy to allow the artifact blob host for agent sessions. Outside the repo; makes a repo-correctness property depend on an infrastructure grant, and the README for that proxy explicitly says to report a denial rather than route around it.",
        "D. ⛔ Refused, recorded so nobody re-proposes it: reconstruct per-package durations from job logs and synthesise run-summary JSON to feed the documented command. Job logs are reachable through MCP, but the summary JSON is never printed to a log (checked: ci.yml has no cat/jq of .turbo/runs) — so this would mean fabricating the generator's input and shipping hand-derived numbers wearing a generated file's provenance, which is the exact failure shape this card exists to remove."
      ],
      "recommendation": "B as the durable answer, with A taken now as the one-shot unblock — and the two are complementary, not competing. Along the four axes: (1) REAL DEMAND, measured not speculative — shard 5 ran its test step at 2.12-2.38x its prediction and shard 6 at 1.54-1.89x on all three green runs above, after twelve-plus wall kills and two merge-queue ejections in a day; the temporary 45-minute wall (#16445) is explicitly held open until this rebalance lands, so doing nothing has a standing cost. (2) LONG-TERM SOUNDNESS, no workarounds — B is the contract-first shape: regenerate where the data already sits, so the refresh stops depending on a channel at all, while A is a patch that re-rots on the same 13-day clock that produced this card and C buys reachability by making correctness depend on a grant nobody in this repo controls. (3) HARDER FOR AN AI TO GET WRONG — B plus the #16465 alarm is declaration-meets-enforcement: the dataset is regenerated by machine and a drift red fires when it rots, instead of a pinned premise nothing re-measures; D is the opposite and is refused outright; and whichever route runs, #16473 must be settled first or the heaviest package in the workspace silently takes its weight from one run while every other package takes a median of three. (4) STARTUP FOCUS, no capability sprawl — B adds no new surface at all: #16464 is already filed, queued and maintainer-directed, so recommending it spends nothing new, whereas C asks for an infrastructure change with no pull behind it and A adds nothing permanent by construction. The one thing I would NOT do is raise MAX_SHARD_OVER_MEAN or MAX_MEASURED_OVER_PREDICTED to make the refresh land quietly; the partitioner's own header calls that the one move that cannot be right, and pin 3c is built to red on exactly that."
    },
    {
      "question": "Ruling 3 asked what to do if the acceptance bound cannot be met without a new file-level slice. That question cannot be answered yet, but it is worth flagging that the answer may well be yes, and the numbers are already visible.",
      "options": [
        "Nothing to decide today — it is decidable only once the dataset is regenerated.",
        "Pre-warn the next round: pin 3c substitutes CLI_MEASURED = 1231.52s and asserts that UNSLICED that value still breaches the floor. Once the refresh writes a real CLI number the pin stops being a substitution and becomes a duplicate of pin 2, and its counterfactual leg will demand a decision if the CLI has come back under the bound on its own."
      ],
      "recommendation": "Flag only, decide nothing. Shard 6 (which carries CLI slice 2/2 and 17 whole packages) ran 1031-1265s against 671s predicted, and shard 5 (CLI slice 1/2 and 11 packages) 1426-1600s against 672s, so the refreshed mean will rise steeply and both the slice count n=2 and the 1.3x bound need re-deriving against the new mean by the arithmetic already written into the partitioner header — never by moving the bound. If n=2 no longer fits, ruling 3 says stop for the seat with the numbers, and that is what the next round should do."
    }
  ],
  "out_of_scope_findings": [
    "filed as #16473: measure-test-shard-timings' buildDataset bypasses its own median merge rule for a file-sharded package — the slice ledger is last-wins across summaries, so @objectstack/cli gets ONE sample regardless of how many runs are fed (proven: 1000s recorded where the median of three fed runs is 600s, while an unsliced control fed the same values correctly records 300s), and slices completed from DIFFERENT runs are summed into a whole-package weight no run ever measured (800s) with skippedIncompleteSlices empty. Directly load-bearing for ruling 1: feeding eighteen summaries from three runs gives every ordinary package a median of three and the heaviest package in the workspace a single observation, silently. Unassigned, unlabelled, for PM triage.",
    "NOT filed, already open as #16222 (2026-09-06): the shard-timings dataset has no scheduled refresh and its documented refresh path is unreachable from an agent container. This round is the second independent reproduction of that card's central measurement, on a different day and a different container, and the second dispatch to be stopped by it. Recording rather than duplicating."
  ]
}


